@@ -85,7 +85,7 @@ In-game settings: **1920x1080 fullscreen**, **no TAA/DLSS/FSR2+/frame generation
 - **replay** (default): the game renders each frame once; the layer submits the same commands again with the other eye's camera. A game at 60 fps gives **60 fps per eye** with both eyes at the same instant. Twice the GPU work. If the game needs GPU results mid-frame, those frames are 2D and the layer falls back to alternate by itself.
 - **alternate**: one game frame per eye; the game must run at 120 fps for 60 per eye.
 - **depth**: for games that cannot use replay and are capped at 60 fps (alternate would give 30 per eye). The game renders one normal image per frame; the layer copies the scene's depth buffer right after the main pass and rebuilds **both eyes** from the image and the depth (60 fps per eye). It also learns how the game's depth maps to distance from samples stored by the patched shaders, so convergence works in the game's units as in the other modes. Limits: small halos where the background is uncovered next to near objects, and the HUD takes the depth of what is behind it. Needs DXVK / VKD3D-Proton (dynamic rendering); the log says when a game is not supported. Frames that draw no 3D scene (menus, loading screens) are shown in 2D instead of being warped by an old depth buffer. Scenes drawn in several stages that clear the depth (several cameras: EDF, Unity games) are combined pixel by pixel, each with its own projection; a depth buffer that is upside down (Unity) is detected by comparing its edges with the image, and a scene shown with black bars (16:10 or 4:3 in a 16:9 window) is placed between them.
-- **hybrid**: alternate and depth together, for the same games (60 fps cap, no replay). Each game frame is rendered for one eye with the real stereo geometry, as in alternate, and the **other eye is rebuilt from that frame's depth**: 60 fps per eye. Half of the images shown are real, and the background uncovered next to near objects comes from the last real image of that eye (one frame old, seen from the right place) instead of being invented: far fewer halos than depth. Menus and loading screens without a 3D scene are shown exactly as in alternate. HUD separation (depth and hybrid, **experimental, off by default**: `hud_separation = 1` in the game's profile): DXVK / VKD3D games draw the finished scene into their back buffer and the HUD on top; the layer finds that image, copies it right after the scene arrived and rebuilds the eyes from the scene alone, with the HUD, crosshair, markers and menus on the screen plane (the log says `HUD separation: on`; it switches itself off in games that do not work that way). In games that record their frames on several threads (EDF 6) the copy can belong to another frame, which gives semi-transparent smudges with movement: leave it off there. Effects that do not write depth (lasers, smoke, some particles) can still be misplaced in the rebuilt eye. Same requirements as depth.
+- **hybrid**: alternate and depth together, for the same games (60 fps cap, no replay). Each game frame is rendered for one eye with the real stereo geometry, as in alternate, and the **other eye is rebuilt from that frame's depth**: 60 fps per eye. Half of the images shown are real, and the background uncovered next to near objects comes from the last real image of that eye (one frame old, seen from the right place) instead of being invented: far fewer halos than depth. Menus and loading screens without a 3D scene are shown exactly as in alternate. HUD separation (depth and hybrid, **experimental, off by default**: `hud_separation = 1` in the game's profile): DXVK / VKD3D games draw the finished scene into their back buffer and the HUD on top; the layer finds that image, copies it right after the scene arrived and rebuilds the eyes from the scene alone, with the HUD, crosshair, markers and menus on the screen plane (the log says `HUD separation: on`). The layer keeps the copies of the last 3 frames and, for each frame, uses the one that matches the image outside the HUD (games that record on several threads, such as EDF 6, present their frames in another order); a frame no copy matches is rebuilt without separation. Translucent HUD panels keep showing the scene through them at its depth. The HUD drawn after the copy is rendered without stereo shift in the real eyes too (games such as EDF 6 draw it with a perspective camera, which put it behind the screen with a different place in each eye), so both eyes see it in the same place. Scene stages: each clear of the scene depth starts a stage with its own projection, and so does going on in another depth image; a pass that goes back to the image of an earlier stage continues that stage (EDF 6 draws a far-away layer in a second depth image, with another camera, in the middle of the main scene). Where several stages have depth, the latest one wins; `depth_layers = nearest` (per game, EDF 6) takes the nearest instead, for far-away layers drawn after the main scene. `flat_menus = 1` (hybrid, per game): screens without a 3D scene (title, menus, loading) are drawn without stereo shift, for games whose 2D menus would otherwise sit far behind the screen (EDF 6). 8-bit and 10-bit back buffers (Unreal games), with or without MSAA. The copy is taken after the first draw into the back buffer (the finished scene); if the copies do not fit the frames, the layer tries after the 2nd, 3rd... draw by itself (log: `now trying the copy after back buffer draw N`), and switches the separation off for a while if none fits. **Ctrl+Shift+Scroll Lock** cycles off → automatic → copy after draw 1, 2 ... 6 → off, for games where the automatic choice is wrong (HUD still double: try the next one); Ctrl+Shift+F9 saves it in the profile (`hud_separation`, `hud_split`). Effects that do not write depth (lasers, smoke, some particles) can still be misplaced in the rebuilt eye. Same requirements as depth.
 
 Replay starts after a **warm-up** of `replay_warmup` seconds (default 8) in alternate: intros, shader compilation and loading screens that would hang in replay get going first (`NV3D_REPLAY_WARMUP=0` disables it).
 
